{"id": "4dcba0bd-b838-4eb9-a866-714c6512a3de", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T07:10:53.542855", "source": "user", "parent_id": "f18deb5c-5dd6-4e73-b7ad-f89ab31ad963", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task for an experiment that investigates how to use autocorrelation analysis to select the optimal cycle length for CycleNet in time series forecasting. Please conduct an initial inspection and report back with ONLY the following items — do not yet produce the full design or any experimental results. (1) Repository check: list the contents of /workspace (top-level dirs/files, any README, docs, or code that mentions CycleNet, time-cycle modeling, period selection, or autocorrelation analysis). Note explicitly that this is a design-only task per track.md and that execution of experiments is not required or expected. (2) Context summary: what is CycleNet (as commonly understood in time-series literature) and what role does its cycle/period hyperparameter play? What does autocorrelation analysis typically reveal about periodicity? (3) Proposed approach: a high-level skeleton of the experiment design answering the research question, including (a) data choices (e.g., synthetic seasonal series with known ground-truth periods, plus 2–3 standard benchmarks such as electricity, weather, traffic, ETT), (b) candidate cycle lengths L to evaluate (e.g., a search grid plus values informed by dominant autocorrelation peaks), (c) how autocorrelation will be computed (ACF on training series, averaged per channel or globally), (d) how candidate L values will be ranked (e.g., argmax of ACF at lag L, or first significant peak above a confidence band), (e) CycleNet training/evaluation protocol (fixed hyperparameters except L, rolling-window or expanding-window validation, metrics MSE/MAE at multiple horizons), and (f) the comparison/control: an oracle selecting the true period, a random-cycle baseline, a uniform/default-cycle baseline, and a grid-search upper bound. (4) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties to resolve before final design: (i) is the goal cycle-length identification accuracy, downstream forecasting improvement, or both? (ii) single-series vs multi-series pool, and whether ACF is computed jointly or per series; (iii) handling of non-stationary or multi-seasonal series where a single dominant period may not exist; (iv) statistical significance/confidence bands for ACF peaks (e.g., 95% Bartlett bound) and tie-breaking; (v) computational budget and how many L candidates we can afford to sweep; (vi) any ambiguity about CycleNet's period parameter semantics from the available code or docs. Return only the inspection report and proposed approach skeleton; the full experimental design will be requested after review.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How can we use autocorrelation analysis to identify the optimal cycle length for CycleNet in time series forecasting?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
